{
  "image_name": "0e63dce45d4c4c863f89b4ea38629058.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how latent cytomegalovirus (CMV) in lymph-node stromal cells may sustain an unusual population of “inflationary” memory CD8+ T cells. A stromal cell carrying latent virus is shown processing CMV protein through the constitutive proteasome and presenting an antigenic peptide on MHC class I. This peptide is recognized by the T-cell receptor on a central-memory-like CD8+ T cell. The interaction is reinforced by several co-stimulatory pathways, including CD27–CD70, OX40–OX40L, and 4-1BB–4-1BBL, indicating that stromal cells provide more than just antigen display. The T cell also receives IL-2 signaling via IL-2Rα, suggesting cytokine support for activation or maintenance.\n\nThe overall message is that antigen presentation within lymph nodes can drive or preserve a specialized TCM population that behaves differently from conventional central memory cells. An arrow points from this inflationary TCM cell toward an inflationary TEM cell, implying differentiation into effector-memory cells over time. In contrast, a conventional TCM cell is shown separately, emphasizing that CMV-specific memory inflation involves a distinct pathway linked to persistent latent antigen presentation in lymphoid tissue.",
